A||||4||Carrolles||99   Olds AM/FM/CD/Cassette in fiero||||9||
Z||000000||Carrolles||03-10-2000||12:34 AM||||I've been wanting to install a CD player in my 85se but I want it to look as much like the stock radio as possible.  My In-laws have a 1999 Olds 98 that has an AM/FM/CD/Cassette player in the dash that looks a lot like our radios.<P>I called around and was told that the unit should fit the Fiero.  I bought one from a large salvage yard (had to order it).  When I got it I noticed that the face plate is just about the same size as our radios but the housing is about 1/2" taller and about 3/4" deeper.  I tried putting it in my Fiero dash and it won't quite fit.  <P>I could trim the top of the opening just under the A/C controls to give a bit more room.  I could also cut the plastic shelf inside the console that the radio actually sits on.  I'd then have to remove the rear support to allow the unit to go deeper into the dash.  I think with these mods the unit just might fit.  I could then trim the faceplate and recover it.<P>A tech at the local radio supply house said our units are 1-1/2 DIN and this is a 2 DIN unit.<P>Have any of you tried this or tried to install a 2 DIN unit in a Fiero?<P>Thanks,<P>Carroll||||reg||9
Z||000001||Monkeyman||03-10-2000||05:55 AM||||A double DIN stereo won't fit in a Fiero.  (Well...I guess it's possible, but you said that you wanted something stock looking so I assume you don't want to tear up your dash trying to get it to fit.)  Sorry you spent your money already.  <A HREF="http://www.aatechnologies.com" TARGET=_blank>www.aatechnologies.com</A>  has used, refurbished factory GM stereos for fairly cheap.  I think cassette decks are going for about $130 and CD players for a little less than twice that.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DJRice||03-10-2000||09:24 AM||||Check Batboys webpage, he added an aftermarket CD stereo, but it was a 1.5 DIN by Pioneer.  I thought it looked real good and no cutting and hacking required.  It might, however, deviate too much from your interest in a stock look.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||03-10-2000||10:10 AM||||Carrolles, this won't help you install that stereo into your car, but if you're a FOCOA member, they had an article a few issues ago about installing later model GM stereos into Fieros. I see three primary options: 1) Make the one you bought fit. It probably can be done, but might take a lot of time cutting and fitting to make it look good. 2) Exchange the stereo for a late model Pontiac factory stereo that will fit (if you stay with Pontiac, then the wiring is easier because the color coding is the same). 3) Sell the one you have and buy an aftermarket CD player like the Pioneer I installed or another brand of 1.5 DIN head unit that will fit. There are about 3 models on the market now, Jensen is one of the other options, although I think the Pioneer is better quality. (Pic of my Batmobile head unit.)<P> <IMG SRC="http://sites.netscape.net/batboybain/batrocks.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||03-10-2000||12:42 PM||||I had actually installed a Jensen AM/FM/CD unit and took it out in order to try the Olds unit.  I'm negotiating with the salvage yard to try and return it for refund or credit.  I don't think I want to butcher my console to make this one work.  I'll probably go for the Jensen unit that has AM/FM/CD/Tape I've seen for $280.<P>Thanks for the replys,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
